Belt drive is actually a really smart call for your situation. Here's why it changes the whole picture:

Belt drive advantages for your build:

Encoder placement becomes trivial — mount it on the output shaft (wheel side) instead of fighting with the motor shaft. Cleaner signal, no magnetic interference from the motor itself.

Reduction ratio gives you torque multiplication. A 2:1 or 3:1 belt ratio means even a high-KV motor becomes viable. Suddenly those drill motors and kart motors enter the conversation seriously.

Heat management improves — motor runs at its efficient RPM range rather than stalling.

What this does to your motor options:

With a 2:1 belt reduction your effective KV halves and torque doubles. With 3:1 it triples. This means:

Hoverboard motor at 2:1 — already 15Nm becomes effectively 30Nm at the wheel. Overkill but incredible.
G30/Mad Max at 2:1 — the higher KV stops being a penalty, torque multiplies significantly.
Kart outrunner motors — if you have anything in the 50-200 KV range, a 3:1 reduction puts them right in the sweet spot.
Drill motors — still probably too high KV even with reduction, and the inrunner form factor makes belt mounting awkward.
The encoder question with belt:

Mount the encoder on the output pulley shaft, which is also your wheel shaft. Clean, simple, and you get true wheel position rather than motor position. MT6701 magnetic encoder with a diametrically magnetized magnet pressed into the shaft end — exactly what the community already does, just on the output shaft instead.

Belt specifics for FFB:

Use GT2 or HTD timing belt, not V-belt. You need zero slip for FFB — any slop or stretch corrupts the force feedback signal. HTD 5M or GT3 is the gold standard for this application. Tension matters a lot — too loose and you get backlash, too tight and you load the bearings.

