Got the full picture now. Here's the clean breakdown:

The actual problem with Xbox One triggers:

The Xbox One controller reports triggers as separate axes via XInput — LT is one axis, RT is another, both 0 to 32767. That's actually fine and correct. The problem is DirectInput — when apps use DirectInput instead of XInput, Windows combines both triggers onto a single Z-axis ranging -32768 to +32767, which is why they cancel each other out.

The solution stack — vJoy + UCR:

UCR (Universal Control Remapper) intercepts Xbox gamepad outputs, sends them to vJoy as inputs, and the game uses vJoy as the steering wheel and pedal devices — keeping triggers as completely separate analog axes. 
Milwaukee Tool

Step by step:

Install vJoy — free virtual joystick driver, creates a virtual HID device Windows sees as a joystick
Install UCR (Universal Control Remapper) — free, maps real controller inputs to vJoy outputs
In UCR: map LT → vJoy Axis 1 (throttle), RT → vJoy Axis 2 (brake) as independent full-range axes
In your sim: bind throttle/brake to the vJoy axes, ignore the real Xbox controller entirely
What games does this work with:

Everything — because the game sees vJoy as a generic joystick, not an Xbox controller at all. No DirectInput vs XInput conflict. Full 0-32767 range on each trigger independently.

