The concept for your build:

Flash the hoverboard mainboard with hoverboard-firmware-hack-FOC, set it to torque mode, then feed it PWM torque commands from the OpenFFBoard STM32 instead of using the Flipsky VESC entirely.

The problem for FFB specifically:

The hoverboard mainboard has no encoder output back to the OpenFFBoard. FFB requires bidirectional communication — the controller needs to send torque commands TO the motor driver AND receive wheel position FROM the encoder. The hoverboard mainboard doesn't report encoder position back over its UART interface in a format OpenFFBoard can use natively.

The bigger problem:

The stock hoverboard controller has no CAN bus. OpenFFBoard talks to VESC and ODrive via CAN. Without CAN you'd need a completely different interface method — PWM torque commands only, which OpenFFBoard supports but with significantly reduced FFB quality compared to CAN torque control.

Bottom line:

Stock hoverboard controller with hacked firmware = possible but inferior FFB quality, no CAN, no encoder feedback to OpenFFBoard, more complexity for worse results than the Flipsky VESC you already have.

Your Flipsky VESC 4.20 is genuinely the better option here. The hoverboard controller is a step backward from what you already own.

