The real problem: The VESC needs to report encoder position back to OpenFFBoard via CAN for FOC torque control to work properly. Without position feedback the VESC can't do closed loop torque mode — it has no idea where the motor is sitting, meaning FFB forces will be wrong or cause runaway.

The practical test path: Use the G30 motor's built in hall sensors as the position feedback via the VESC — that's already there, costs nothing, no additional encoder needed. Hall sensors give lower resolution than a proper encoder but are perfectly sufficient to verify FFB is working before buying an encoder.

So: G30 hall sensors → VESC FOC → CAN → OpenFFBoard → FFB confirmed working → then upgrade to proper encoder later.

