What changed inside the APK (verified by decompiling the rebuilt classes.dex)
com.ik.simwheel.MainActivity now overrides two activity-level methods that fire before Flutter sees the event:

dispatchKeyEvent(KeyEvent) — intercepts hardware-controller button presses
dispatchGenericMotionEvent(MotionEvent) — intercepts analog axes (L2/R2 triggers, sticks, D-pad hat)
Both forward to com.ik.simwheel.bridge.ControllerBridge. If the event came from SOURCE_GAMEPAD/SOURCE_JOYSTICK/SOURCE_DPAD (i.e. a paired Bluetooth/USB controller — exactly what you asked for, not on-screen), it's swallowed. Everything else falls through to the original FlutterActivity untouched, so all your existing settings, UI, gyro, throttle/brake sliders, button binding screens, etc. still work normally.

What the bridge sends to the PC receiver
A background thread (started on onPostResume) opens a UDP socket and sends packets to port 4567 — same protocol as the original app:

"throttle": analog 0.0–1.0 from AXIS_RTRIGGER / AXIS_GAS (R2)
"brake": analog 0.0–1.0 from AXIS_LTRIGGER / AXIS_BRAKE (L2)
"1"–"29": booleans for face buttons (A/B/X/Y), shoulders (L1/R1), digital L2/R2, Select/Start, thumb sticks, Mode, generic buttons 1–11, and D-pad (25=UP, 26=DOWN, 27=LEFT, 28=RIGHT)
"phoneName": identifies the phone, so the receiver's security prompt names it correctly
Packets go to the broadcast address and to the last receiver IP (so it works whether you've already paired or are still discovering). Sender is rate-capped to ~125 Hz, with a 20 Hz keepalive.

