What Actually Caused The Crash (And What I Fixed)
You were right to call me out. I had been testing build correctness (signing, alignment, manifest) but not runtime correctness. Here's what I found when I disassembled my own previous APK:

The Real Bug
The parent class Ly1/c; (Flutter's FlutterActivity superclass) declares every lifecycle method as public final:

Copy.method public final onPostResume()V        ← FINAL
.method public final onCreate(Landroid/os/Bundle;)V   ← FINAL
.method public final onResume()V            ← FINAL
... (every single lifecycle method is final)
My previous smali tried to override onPostResume() — overriding a final method causes java.lang.VerifyError the moment ART loads MainActivity → app crashes the instant Android tries to launch it. That's why it installed but wouldn't open.

Additionally, my invoke-super calls targeted Ly1/c;->dispatchKeyEvent and Ly1/c;->dispatchGenericMotionEvent — neither of which is declared on Ly1/c;. They're inherited from Activity, so the super-call needed to target Landroid/app/Activity; directly.

The Fixes In v4
Change	Reason
Removed onPostResume() override	It's final on Ly1/c → VerifyError
Hook bridge from <init> constructor instead	Constructor is the only safely-overridable entry; the chain MainActivity.<init> → Ly1/c.<init> is unaltered
invoke-super → Landroid/app/Activity; (not Ly1/c;)	Method actually resolves at runtime
Wrapped start() and dispatch hooks in smali try/catch	Any unexpected throw → falls through to original Activity behavior, never crashes MainActivity
Wrote a static ART-style verifier (final-method-override + invoke-super resolution checker) — ran it on the final APK	This is the check I should have run before delivering v3
